<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mississippian culture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mississippian culture was a Native American mound-building civilisation that flourished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> valley and the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from about 800 to 1600 CE. Based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> farming, it formed chiefdoms centred on towns with large earthen platform mounds, the greatest being Cahokia near modern St Louis. Many later peoples of the South-East, among them the Choctaw, Chickasaw and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>, descended from Mississippian communities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture.jpg" alt="Mississippian culture" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Willoughby_disc,_engraved_paint_palette,_Mississippian_tradition,_Moundville,_Alabama,_1300-1450_AD,_ploughed_up_1860,_shale_-_Native_American_collection_-_Peabody_Museum,_Harvard_University_-_DSC06065.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
